\f0\fs22 \cf2 He spent the afternoon deciding who to ask, and every name he considered was wrong.\
Scientists would say yes. That was not a criticism; it was their function, and Hardy had never once resented a man for doing the thing he was paid to do. Doctors would say yes, and would say it publicly, and would say it in a way that made anyone who disagreed sound like a murderer. Ministers would leak, not from conviction but from the ordinary human need to be the first at a dinner party to know something. The board would want a briefing, and a briefing was a document with names on it, and the names would be the names of the men in that morning\u8217 ?s meeting, and every one of them had a wife.\
What Hardy wanted was an answer he could hold up. He wanted it produced by people with no stake in the outcome, no standing to be quoted, and no idea what they were looking at.\
So he took the finance department, and from the finance department he took the juniors.\
There were twelve of them: accountants, economists, three finance advisers and a woman from Treasury Modelling whose job title ran to fourteen words and described, in essence, the making of graphs. They were the people who spent their days building models nobody read, of futures nobody intended to have, for reports that existed so that a decision already taken could be described afterwards as evidence-based. They were good at it. They were good at it the way a well-made hinge is good: silently, and without any expectation of being looked at.\
They were told they had been selected. The word was chosen with some care, and it worked exactly as intended. Four of them assumed it was a promotion. Two rang their mothers. One, a young man named\u8212 ?but that is the whole point, and we shall come to it\u8212 ?one of them brought along a folder of proposals he had been carrying for four years, waiting for a senior person to be trapped in a room with him.\
They were collected in the dark, in three cars, and not told where they were going.\
The site was in the Pale, though nobody called it that then; it was simply the part of the city where the labs were, because land there was cheap and neighbours were not the sort who wrote letters. The lift went down for a long time. It went down long enough that two of the juniors got the giggles, and then stopped, the way people stop when a lift keeps going.\
Fifty metres, and then the pressure doors, which sighed. Then a corridor.\
The corridor was where Hardy stopped and read a sign. He stood in front of it in a suit worth more than the annual maintenance budget of the level he was standing on, in a building he owned outright, and he read the sign twice and then took the left-hand branch, and one of the accountants behind him noticed, and filed it away, and mentioned it to nobody for the rest of his life, which was that day.\
Somebody took a photograph of the corridor and was asked not to.\
The room was a conference room. Bad light, a whiteboard nobody had cleaned properly, a jug of water and twelve chairs, which meant that Hardy stood.\
\u8220 ?I don\u8217 ?t know any of your names,\u8221 ? he said, \u8220 ?and I have declined to be told them. Please don\u8217 ?t tell me. You have been brought here precisely because I don\u8217 ?t know you.\u8221 ?\
They took this as a security formality, which is the most sensible thing to take it as.\
He gave them the question. What happens to the economy if disease ends. Not slows, not becomes manageable: ends. He gave them access to a data feed that would answer any question they cared to put to it and would not permit anything to leave the room. He gave them seven hours.\
He did not tell them why seven. One of them asked, in the reasonable manner of a person hoping to be granted eight, and Hardy said, \u8220 ?Operational,\u8221 ? and the young man wrote operational in his notebook and underlined it.\
Then came the questions from the floor, and there were nine of them, and every one was professional, and every one was beside the point.\
Was this a memo or a deck. Was there a template. Were they assuming current tax settings, or should they model reform. Was the audience the board or the regulator, because those were different documents. Should they present a base case only, or a base case with sensitivities. Would there be a version-control convention. What was the citation standard. What happened if they needed longer.\
And then, from the back, the ninth question, which was about lunch.\
Hardy brightened. He had thought about lunch that morning for a good deal longer than he had thought about anything else, and had settled it personally, and it pleased him enormously to be able to say so. There was a catering order. It was a very good one. There would be a hot service at one and something lighter later, and the coffee was the coffee from the executive floor and not the coffee from anywhere else, and he had checked.\
They were delighted. Several of them said so.\
He wished them well and left them to it, and the door closed, and it was the last time that door opened while anybody in the room was in a position to notice.}